WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
Blades of Steel, fight for victory in the hottest hockey game on ice.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:29.860
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:31.140
I'm Mike.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.240
I'm Sean. I'm Joe.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:34.840
And I'm Sam.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:41.560
You know, guys, last week I said at the very end of the episode that we were going to give

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:46.320
Blades of Steel a fair shot, just like we gave Bionic Commando last week.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.300
And I didn't really know what I meant by that.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.080
Like, that doesn't, that doesn't, that kind of sounds a little suspicious of me.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.880
Yeah, I would say, Mike, I was going to say something.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.960
Uh, but it sounds like, and it's just the way that you have conducted the Essential Games

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:06.800
list, uh, these past 140 something episodes, uh, you try and put your finger on the scales

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:07.660
a little too much.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:15.580
And, uh, I think that just by virtue of being, uh, a man who loves democracy, it's never something

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:17.300
that I've been a very big fan of.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.940
And you couldn't be more timely with that with the election right around the corner.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:28.320
Well, I just thought that, you know, if, uh, Konami doesn't reach out to us and kind of,

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.000
kind of line our pockets a little bit, we don't vote at Essential.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:34.420
Isn't that what we do with, isn't that how Bionic Commando got on?

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:37.700
Yeah. Uh, well, Konami actually funds the whole show.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.140
We should just say that up front.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:45.160
Like, we received a very generous, uh, you know, startup investor from an angel investor

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.280
from Konami.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.740
Uh, and that's how we've been running the whole show.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.700
So what I really meant to say when, before this whole thing got derailed was that, are

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.400
we just, are we giving sports games a fair shot in general on the NES?

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:03.840
Or do we, do we see sports titles and we're like, oh, we know this one's not, not happening.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.220
And we've gone on record as saying that there would never be a sports game on the Essential

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:13.660
Games list, but I always still try and look at them in a way that does give them a shot.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:15.960
Yeah, I'm kind of in the same boat.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:20.280
I have a hard time seeing that happening, but I'm, I'm for it.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:25.700
But also it's just, uh, I'm probably not the guy to, to be, to be judging sports games.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:26.980
So yeah, I do my best.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:32.060
I would argue that there already is an Essential Sports game in Duck Hunt.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:33.620
It's a good plus.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:34.960
And Mike Tyson's Punch-Out.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.400
Oh yeah, that one too, does.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.340
It's more, yeah, Punch-Out is definitely a better example.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:44.760
But I think that like for sports games to be successful on the NES, they can't try to make,

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:46.460
to be like the whole game.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:52.320
They should focus on like one aspect of a game or of a sport.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.780
You know, like Punch-Out isn't really, it's more of like an action puzzle game.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.980
And you're not like going through boxing training and stuff.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.120
It's a rhythm game, Sam.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.920
Yeah, yeah. Rhythm puzzle something.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:09.080
I don't know. So this is our second hockey game.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:14.180
I think before Blades of Steel, we just had ice hockey, which came to us directly from Nintendo.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:19.480
So this is Konami's shot at upstaging Nintendo on what it takes to make a hockey game.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:24.220
Hockey, of course, just ended like last month.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.900
The Tampa Bay Lightning, aka the New York Rangers Part 2, won the Stanley Cup.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.380
So in many ways, the Rangers also won.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:34.180
They also won the Stanley Cup in a way.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:39.120
I was happy to see some old teammates lift the cup.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:44.560
But we all know that next year, the Buffalo Bills will win the Stanley Cup championship for sure.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:46.320
The Stanley Cup.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:51.460
Anyway, well, yeah, after they win the Super Bowl, they'll just take the Stanley Cup too.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.280
They'll just go for the 14.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:54.820
Josh Allen cannot be stopped.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:04:00.000
There you go. Anyway, I think it's time we start talking about Blades of Steel.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:12.200
You know, the difference here, the immediate difference from ice hockey is that all the teams are fictional, but they're based on they're based out of real Canadian and American cities.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:17.540
And now that I'm reading that sentence that I wrote, I don't think that was true in ice hockey.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:19.400
Did you actually get to play as the New York Rangers?

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:24.000
I don't I don't remember if you could be.

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:30.460
I think that. They were they were teams based off of nationalities.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:32.200
It was an American. Oh, right, right.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:33.660
That one was. Yes.

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:38.180
So you were able to. This is this is the first game that it cities.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.860
And they only have eight of them, I think, or 12 or I don't know.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:46.220
But it's just it's it's it's eight.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:47.700
There are four Canadian teams.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:49.080
There's four American teams.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:54.100
And they try and get the colors right for the most part.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:58.740
But in no way is this an NHL or NHLPA sanctioned game.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:04.840
But yeah, since Buffalo is not included, that's already points off.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:06.380
Right. Absolutely.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:08.860
It's OK. We'll get into that.

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:15.460
Yeah. I forgot that ice hockey included like Soviet Russia and and like, no, they said that,

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.540
too. Like, I'm not I'm not saying that.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:19.340
No, it's a side. I think West Germany.

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:21.360
Right. One of those Germany.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:25.080
Yeah. So that was strange and definitely a relic.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:31.900
So I thought that was cool. But, you know, playing as cities maybe feels a little more like you don't just always choose

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:35.160
USA. I always chose USA when we played ice hockey.

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:37.120
Yeah. So now I have more choices.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:38.300
Now you choose New York.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:39.560
Yeah, I choose New York.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:44.040
I can't wait for the version where I can just specifically pick my town in Queens.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:45.940
And be like, yeah, I'm.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:48.020
That's Urban Champion.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:49.720
Yeah. Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.080
Right. Exactly.

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:55.040
So you get a choice between exhibition or tournament mode.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:01.980
And, you know, I just want to say off the bat, I have a beef with the the choices here.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:04.200
So it says exhibition or tournament.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:06.820
OK, I'm totally fine with just having two choices.

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:12.140
That's normal. I'm not expecting some like new way to play hockey.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:17.420
But you choose exhibition and then you choose your team and then you choose the other team.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.720
And then you have to hit the A button again.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.360
Oh, I knew you were going to bring this up.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:24.580
I knew you were going to bring this up.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:25.600
Just for no reason at all.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:27.700
You have to. It's just confirming, Mike.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:29.140
I don't get it, though.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:30.160
You've already confirmed.

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:31.420
You've picked everything.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:33.060
I think. I mean.

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:35.080
I appreciate having the like.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:36.080
Oh, are you sure?

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:39.240
Yeah. It's it's trying to make sure like you're being responsible.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:40.900
It's like, OK, you hit it.

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:42.940
But we're going to give you one last chance to back out if you want.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:46.420
Otherwise, like it's like this is what you're playing or you got to get up and hit the reset button.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:50.820
So you have to wait like a full minute before you actually start playing.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:52.700
So get ready.

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:53.940
Yeah. Exactly.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:55.760
You have like this whole cut scene beforehand.

00:06:56.260 --> 00:07:00.660
I don't know. There's something about the flow of things that it just felt off to me.

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:06.540
Every time I started a new match, I had to press that extra A press when it wasn't necessary.

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:08.600
Well, if it was just a half A press.

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:10.280
But whatever. Yeah, I get it.

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:17.340
Yes. You know, and then there's also on, you know, on the positive side, when you choose exhibition

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:21.540
or tournament or whatever, there's a nice little Zamboni driving around.

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:23.340
Zambonis are always cool.

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:32.720
I appreciate the extra touch there that they didn't just like have a blue Final Fantasy like menu screen where you just like are hung on that.

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:38.540
And instead, you get to see the ring and watch the Zamboni kind of satisfyingly drive across all of the ring, too.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.820
It doesn't just scroll from right to left and then back over.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:44.740
It actually does like the whole ice.

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:55.120
One other thing that I'd like to bring up, it's always like a thing that I appreciate, is they have contextualized difficulty settings where it's not just easy, medium, and hard.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:57.740
It's junior, college, and pro.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:05.860
So that was, I appreciate that because, you know, there's the juniors and then there's college.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:07.600
Then there's professional hockey.

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:08.760
So that's always fun.

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:18.380
Yeah, you're right. I never thought about that, but in a way, I'm playing as NYU when I pick the college mode and pick New York as opposed to playing as the New York Rangers.

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:21.820
True, yeah. I'd love to see an NYU hockey team.

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:27.160
Well, who would be like the de facto New York college for hockey?

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:31.280
You're right. Other than the Tisch School of Arts.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:36.260
Anyway. All right.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.140
Let's talk about the actual game, though.

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:43.200
Just, you know, for me, some general observations and then everybody else can kind of go around, too.

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:45.980
Right away, the voice effects.

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:47.260
Very cool.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:49.540
Clear for the most part.

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:55.200
Apparently, some people have problems with hearing what he says when you get a pass.

00:08:55.400 --> 00:09:00.240
But I heard pass, so I could care less what else he was trying to say.

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:02.700
But do we have a verdict on what he was trying to say there?

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:04.620
Get the pass. It's a pass.

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:07.000
Okay. It's a pass.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:09.360
Yeah. I guess that was cool.

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:11.560
And it's like, oh, the announcer is talking.

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:13.520
No, it's everything. It's a every pass.

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:15.300
Yeah. Do we need it every time?

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:16.680
I mean, I'm playing the game.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:17.860
I know I'm passing.

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:22.380
I would have appreciated it more.

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:27.100
Go ahead. I would have appreciated it more if it was just like, with the shot or something like

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:28.700
that. Not every pass.

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:29.800
I don't know. Go ahead.

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:34.500
I'm sorry, Joe. No, I was just saying, I do like the voice effect, you know, even at the beginning when

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:36.080
it goes, blades of steel.

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:40.680
I mean, I can't do it as badly as they do it, but it still sounds pretty good for the

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.700
NES. Mike can pixelize that.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:44.600
Yeah. I'll do my best.

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:48.080
And here's another thing, too, just since we're talking about the voice effects thing.

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:52.040
Sean, do they actually ever say when you take a shot or is it just when you pass that

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:53.340
they have voice effects? It's just passing.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:54.540
I don't remember that.

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:58.680
Yeah. Why wouldn't they? They have the main title screen.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:00.120
They have face-offs.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:02.840
They have passing.

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:04.320
They have fights.

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:08.640
And they might say penalty shot, but I don't think so.

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:14.480
And that's it. Well, I mean, if that's not the whole game of hockey, you know, who needs shooting?

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:17.440
Why even, you know, they probably said, why bother with the voice effects?

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:19.700
I love it, yeah. That's the least exciting part.

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:21.800
When someone scores, nobody cares.

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:22.980
I hate scoring, yeah.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:25.100
Passing, that's where you're, oh my God, you see that pass?

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:29.860
Nine out of ten hockey fans will tell you that during a power play, nobody shoots anyway.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:31.300
Yeah, it's true. Just wheel.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:34.460
Right. So there's that.

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:38.900
And then another general observation, and maybe I should have saved this for later on,

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:43.900
but, you know, it's nice that they contextualize junior, college, and pro,

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:47.580
but I found all three computers to be pretty easy in this game.

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:48.700
Even pro.

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:54.800
I won pretty convincingly on every single game I played.

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:57.260
And that enhanced my enjoyment of this game.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:04.320
So I, okay, so before we get into, like, how good the computer plays this version of hockey,

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:07.260
we should probably get into what this version of hockey is.

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:09.520
Yes, because it is a little different.

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:20.720
Yeah. So, hockey so far, I think, has done the worst job in these early iterations of sports games

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:24.420
of, like, simulating the rules, aside from maybe, like, ten-yard fight,

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:26.980
because we haven't really had another football game yet.

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:34.440
But, so in this game, they, like, there's no offside, which is a pretty,

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:37.180
it's a pretty important part of the game.

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:43.200
There is, they do do icing, which was surprising for me,

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:46.300
when you just clear the puck immediately down the ice.

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:50.540
They have an interesting way of doing penalties, though,

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:53.060
because there's only one kind of, there's two kinds of penalties.

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:58.840
Getting in a fight, and it's not really just that fighting is a penalty,

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:00.440
it's the way that they enforce the penalty.

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:05.040
And I love this, and I really, I wish that there was an alternate reality

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:06.660
in which this was just hockey was played.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:12.260
But whoever loses the fight gets penalized, and the winner gets to stay on,

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:13.960
and they don't even, like, stop the game.

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:20.240
Like, you just continue to skate while the refs come and just take him away to the cage.

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:25.240
And, like, that's just how you get, that's just how you get scoring opportunities.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:29.500
The other one is that if that happens in front of the goalie,

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:32.000
you get, like, some weird soccer-style penalty shot.

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:35.420
But that fight mechanic and the way that they penalize those,

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:37.700
I love it, and I want to see it in action.

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:40.180
Yeah, I feel like they were just like, well, we have to,

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:42.440
fighting's a big part of hockey, we have to include it.

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:44.900
But then, like, from the video game mechanic side of it,

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:46.880
like, well, you need to have an incentive to win the fight.

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:50.700
You know? So, like, they're like, oh, let's just change the rules a little bit.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:59.760
Yeah. It was by far, like, the games that I played while I was playing Blades of Steel

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:03.960
were probably the most fights I've ever seen in a hockey game

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:08.340
because it was, I just loved that aspect of it so much.

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:11.580
It's also, like, three bumps and a fight starts.

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:15.160
You know? I don't know any hockey game that would get escalated that quickly.

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:18.060
But, yeah, you do find yourself fighting a lot.

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:22.360
And if we're being honest here, this is some of the best fighting,

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:27.180
you know, like, side-scrolling fighting we've seen in an NES game yet.

00:13:27.460 --> 00:13:29.380
Like, this is good combat.

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:32.420
You're able to, like, choose to hit in the chest or the head.

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:33.760
You can choose to block.

00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:36.340
Like, it felt a little proto Street Fighter.

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:39.860
I mean, I feel like it felt a little bit more, like,

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:42.020
on the level with Urban Champion.

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:43.720
But I agree.

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:46.380
I agree that it is better than Urban Champion.

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:49.620
And that's weird that it's a hockey game that's better than Urban Champion.

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:52.400
Yeah, there's just, like, one little gameplay element

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:53.920
that's better than that entire game.

00:13:54.220 --> 00:13:59.020
Yeah. Sean, you mentioned no offside rule, and that is true.

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:03.420
But were you able to, like, pass in a way that would have been illegal?

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:08.040
Like, I found that I actually couldn't pass to somebody

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:09.860
who was in the next zone over.

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:14.060
It just, like, wouldn't let me do that because it would pass.

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:20.120
What I'll say is that while it's really hard to choose a specific player

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:23.380
that you want to pass to, you do have a little bit of input.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:28.220
But regardless of that, you can cross the blue – I'm sorry.

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:31.620
You can cross the offensive zone – into the offensive zone

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:36.060
while you already have guys over there, which should not happen.

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:38.420
Right, that's true. Yes, that's what I'm not thinking of.

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:40.900
Yeah, and that's why offsides is not in the game.

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:45.840
But, like, just going back to icing, I was just surprised

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:48.280
that they put that in there at all because it just seems so unlikely

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:49.120
that it'll happen.

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:51.900
And when it did happen, I was just caught off guard

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:55.200
because they actually, like, brought the face off into the other zone.

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:56.300
It was just – it was weird.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:15:00.260
It was weird. Was there icing in – there wasn't icing in ice hockey.

00:15:00.460 --> 00:15:01.640
I don't recall.

00:15:01.700 --> 00:15:02.960
I don't think so.

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:05.540
I recall ice hockey being, like, lawless.

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:07.580
Yeah, pretty much. Like, there were barely any rules.

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:10.120
It was just, like, they figured out that – they programmed it

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:12.580
so that if the puck went into the net, it counted as a goal.

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:15.280
But otherwise, not many other things were enforced.

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:18.320
You know, how did you guys feel, though, about, like,

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:21.500
how the players control on the ice, you know, like, skating?

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:24.220
Like, was it slippery?

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:27.520
Was it too much friction?

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:30.780
Did you enjoy, like, controlling all your different players

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:32.140
and just playing hockey?

00:15:32.140 --> 00:15:35.700
Was that fun? I didn't think it really felt like ice too much,

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:38.320
but I thought that everyone was really responsive

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:40.860
and very arcade-like in the way that they played,

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:43.040
so I thought that it was successful in that way.

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:47.280
Yeah, I felt like you had the – you know, I agree it didn't feel like ice,

00:15:47.320 --> 00:15:49.700
but I feel like it was – it's not supposed to.

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:53.320
You know, if you're playing as professional hockey players,

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:57.460
then it's not going to be super slippery like a Mario ice would be.

00:15:58.180 --> 00:16:00.700
So, I mean, it didn't bother me. Ice is not supposed to feel like ice.

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:02.620
I appreciate that. You know what I mean.

00:16:02.980 --> 00:16:03.900
No, I know what you mean.

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:05.360
If you're skating, yeah, it's not going to –

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:07.360
Mario ice is there as, like, a challenge,

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:10.200
but, like, being on ice in this isn't supposed to be a challenge.

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:15.720
I will say that from my memory of ice hockey, which is kind of foggy,

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:19.740
I think that it did a better job of feeling like hockey,

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:23.920
like, in just, like, that moment-to-moment, like, moving in a direction,

00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:29.720
whereas this felt a bit more one-to-one and less momentum-based,

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:31.300
like I'll use that phrase again.

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:37.460
But it didn't – it wasn't something that called attention to itself.

00:16:37.780 --> 00:16:39.220
Yeah, Sean, I think you're spot on there.

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:42.120
Ice hockey had more flair to it, you know.

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:46.480
You were able to choose the types of player for your team, you know,

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:50.520
between, like, three different guys, a bulky guy, a skinny guy, a tall guy.

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:52.360
Like, you were able to choose your players,

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:56.400
and it was a lot more momentum-based in the sense that, like,

00:16:56.400 --> 00:16:59.200
if you started skating in a direction and skated long enough,

00:16:59.320 --> 00:17:01.740
you wouldn't be able to just stop on a dime.

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:04.360
So you did have to think about how you were skating around on the ice.

00:17:04.360 --> 00:17:08.980
And it had, like, a, you know, a cute Nintendo way of presenting hockey.

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:11.360
But I'd argue that Blades of Steel,

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:15.980
even if it doesn't control quite like how we would expect ice hockey to control,

00:17:16.220 --> 00:17:19.300
it's better for these purposes of playing hockey.

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:25.040
And it was easier for me to be really good at playing hockey in Blades of Steel

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:30.520
comparatively to ice hockey because of the way that you're able to, like,

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:32.520
have more control over your players.

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:35.500
And you can kind of, you can get a better idea of who you are

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:36.400
because they're glowing.

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:39.520
And when you pass, it's like, it doesn't just,

00:17:39.800 --> 00:17:42.240
the puck doesn't just kind of, like, float out into the ether.

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:44.040
It goes to another one of your players.

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:48.200
Like, those things, the way that the game kind of assists you,

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:53.120
made it easier and more enjoyable to play Blades of Steel for me than ice hockey did.

00:17:53.540 --> 00:17:58.580
Yeah, there were still a couple things that I would have hoped that they'd have solved by now.

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:02.400
I mean, I think that ice hockey was still 88,

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:06.280
so there wasn't really much time between these two to make improvements.

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:10.180
I know they're not made by the same people,

00:18:10.220 --> 00:18:16.420
but the one thing that I was still a little annoyed by was the way in which you, like,

00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:17.660
choose where you're shooting,

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:25.800
and that's just sort of waiting until a constantly scrolling cursor picks a spot for you

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:27.000
when you used to time it correctly.

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:31.060
I think, I mean, I think it worked a little differently in ice hockey,

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:32.580
but it's sort of the same principle.

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:40.800
And also the fact that you are simultaneously controlling your goalie,

00:18:41.740 --> 00:18:46.540
which is kind of difficult when you're also trying to retrieve the puck from the slot

00:18:46.540 --> 00:18:50.840
and, like, stop constant, like, shot after shot after shot.

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:55.800
It's just a little mind-bendy to try and do that, like, both of those things at once.

00:18:56.580 --> 00:19:02.980
But all in all, I do think that it is an improvement in the moment-to-moment gameplay from ice hockey.

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:04.780
It's an interesting dilemma, though, right?

00:19:04.900 --> 00:19:09.700
Like, I totally get that it's upsetting that you can't choose your own shot,

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:14.720
but in a way, it helps to have it that way because when you're playing as the goalie,

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:19.780
it does help to know where the shot is going to be to seamlessly change

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:25.640
from being one of the defensemen to being the goalie because you can kind of say, like,

00:19:25.740 --> 00:19:29.440
your eyes just react quicker to that than, say, you would be able to

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:31.920
if there was no indicator of where it was being shot.

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:34.900
Yeah, and it is helpful that it's not just for your shot.

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:38.000
It's also telling you where the opponent's shot will be,

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:40.220
so at least it sort of levels the playing field there.

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:43.680
Right, and maybe that's something they could have turned off for pro mode,

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:48.020
but then it would have just been a huge disadvantage to the player with no way to shoot,

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:50.500
to know where you're going to shoot.

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:56.780
From what I understand, though, with the shots that the opponent's team take

00:19:56.780 --> 00:20:01.800
is that your goalie can't just, like, sit on the puck and force a faceoff.

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:04.740
He always just, like, if I didn't choose to pass it,

00:20:05.240 --> 00:20:07.920
he would just pass it after a certain amount of time ran out.

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:10.040
Is that true? I think so, yeah.

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:14.740
I remember just because, not to go a little off topic here,

00:20:14.900 --> 00:20:21.560
but it's really hard to get the puck out when the computer opponent

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:23.420
will just stand in front of your goalie,

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:29.820
and for some reason it's hard to change who you're going to pass to from the goalie,

00:20:29.820 --> 00:20:33.960
so I just kept passing it to the guy that was taking shots at me.

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:41.160
But, like, and if I waited too long to try and, like, find a clear zone,

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:42.940
I would just do it anyway.

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:48.640
So, you are correct that it will not, you can't freeze the puck in this game.

00:20:49.660 --> 00:20:54.560
I was going to say that I think, I didn't play the two-player mode.

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:57.360
I don't know if any of you guys did, but...

00:20:57.360 --> 00:21:02.180
I did not. I'm wondering if the way that the kind of inconvenient...

00:21:02.180 --> 00:21:04.660
The inconvenient way that the goalie moves,

00:21:04.900 --> 00:21:08.180
where you're moving your character and the goalie at the same time,

00:21:08.740 --> 00:21:12.160
if that is kind of beneficial for two-player mode,

00:21:12.320 --> 00:21:15.700
because then, if you could control your goalie really well,

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:18.180
it's probably not that hard to block a lot of shots,

00:21:19.300 --> 00:21:21.080
whereas the two-player mode might kind of, like,

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:23.600
make it easier to present more opportunities to score

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:26.200
if it's, like, a little bit trickier.

00:21:26.680 --> 00:21:28.500
Yeah, there's definitely a balance there.

00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:30.740
I'll say, I'll agree with that.

00:21:30.900 --> 00:21:33.500
I just think it's the wrong way to balance it,

00:21:33.520 --> 00:21:35.340
because it's just kind of frustrating.

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:38.780
Yeah, I agree with that. Yeah, I'll say, I played two-player mode,

00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:42.320
I think when, last time I played it was when I was, like, eight years old,

00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:43.680
so I don't really remember,

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:46.300
but I did used to play this game a lot, and two-player...

00:21:46.300 --> 00:21:48.960
Oh, you did? Yeah, actually, this is one of the few on the NES,

00:21:49.060 --> 00:21:50.300
that my brother's a big hockey guy,

00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:51.580
so this was, like, his game,

00:21:51.940 --> 00:21:53.460
and he used to always make me play it,

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:54.620
and probably usually beat me,

00:21:54.760 --> 00:21:57.260
but I did used to play this as a kid a lot.

00:21:57.360 --> 00:21:58.880
It was, like, this and, like, Super Mario Bros.

00:21:58.940 --> 00:22:02.120
3 were, like, my first, like, NES games I used to play all the time,

00:22:02.480 --> 00:22:04.380
which is weird, because I'm not a hockey guy myself,

00:22:04.400 --> 00:22:07.080
but, yeah, just bringing that up to tell you

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:08.420
that I still can't answer your question.

00:22:11.240 --> 00:22:13.580
Yeah, it left me wondering, you know,

00:22:15.080 --> 00:22:17.180
what playing two-player mode against, you know,

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:18.880
like, I don't know what success you guys had

00:22:18.880 --> 00:22:20.000
against the computer players,

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:23.740
but two-player mode is definitely a,

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:26.340
you know, would be the preferred way to play

00:22:26.340 --> 00:22:29.180
any of these sports games, in my opinion,

00:22:29.780 --> 00:22:33.000
because there's an extra element there

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:35.780
that these NES games just aren't quite equipped

00:22:35.780 --> 00:22:39.860
to handle specifically sports games,

00:22:39.980 --> 00:22:42.500
like, things... the way that we think about these

00:22:42.500 --> 00:22:44.620
and play these games,

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:46.980
where, like, two players can get inside

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:48.080
of each other's heads,

00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:52.440
and, like, it's very easy for me to figure out,

00:22:52.520 --> 00:22:55.080
like, oh, okay, like, if I go around the net,

00:22:55.280 --> 00:22:58.340
the computers usually, like, fall for the same trick,

00:22:58.400 --> 00:22:59.860
and I can pass to this guy,

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:01.780
and there's a good chance that he'll be open or whatever.

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:04.560
But, like, a player would be able to adapt to that over time,

00:23:04.660 --> 00:23:05.480
you know, another person.

00:23:05.480 --> 00:23:09.420
So, not that I found any, like, amazing cheats

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:10.520
against the computer.

00:23:10.700 --> 00:23:12.180
I just think, in general,

00:23:12.300 --> 00:23:14.480
the computer difficulty was lacking in the game.

00:23:15.540 --> 00:23:18.220
But, you know, I think this is just another reason

00:23:18.220 --> 00:23:21.340
why these games are better with two players

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:23.660
is because you have something

00:23:23.660 --> 00:23:25.240
that the computer just can't offer.

00:23:25.240 --> 00:23:27.460
Yeah. You know? Yeah, I think that where...

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:28.840
That's it. Yeah, go ahead.

00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:31.480
I think that where the single player would kind of,

00:23:31.660 --> 00:23:33.920
like, once they start doing this,

00:23:34.040 --> 00:23:36.120
I think where it would start to kind of shine a little more

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:37.560
is when they start adding, like, a season

00:23:37.560 --> 00:23:39.180
or, like, a career mode or something,

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:40.680
where it's like, okay, now you have a reason

00:23:40.680 --> 00:23:43.600
to play single player because you're working to get to the Stanley Cup.

00:23:43.620 --> 00:23:45.860
And I guess you could do a, like, tournament in here, sort of,

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:46.980
but it's not really the same.

00:23:47.800 --> 00:23:48.880
So, like, in this, yeah,

00:23:48.960 --> 00:23:51.500
I think that the only draw to, like,

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:53.420
maybe, like, an older crowd

00:23:53.420 --> 00:23:55.740
is playing against a friend

00:23:55.740 --> 00:23:57.580
because then you actually have real competition.

00:23:58.120 --> 00:23:58.960
Yeah, and they do.

00:23:59.140 --> 00:24:00.600
I mean, they do have the...

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:02.500
They have the tournament

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:05.680
and you play for the quote-unquote cup.

00:24:06.160 --> 00:24:09.840
But, no, there's nothing for...

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:13.160
I think the fact that I really like sports games

00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:15.640
is because it has those season modes

00:24:15.640 --> 00:24:19.300
and these games don't really do it for me that much

00:24:19.300 --> 00:24:22.120
because of that, like, extra layer.

00:24:23.540 --> 00:24:25.800
Because I can deal with a lot of these faults

00:24:25.800 --> 00:24:29.940
if you can sort of not just tell the story of a season,

00:24:29.960 --> 00:24:33.780
but just, like, have that upper-level play

00:24:33.780 --> 00:24:35.120
of managing a season.

00:24:35.120 --> 00:24:36.520
And that's a...

00:24:36.520 --> 00:24:38.620
We still haven't had that.

00:24:38.900 --> 00:24:43.620
Yeah. And, you know, the reason I keep talking about, like, computers

00:24:43.620 --> 00:24:47.240
and just, like, the way that you score in this game

00:24:47.240 --> 00:24:50.600
is because I found that a lot of times, you know,

00:24:50.600 --> 00:24:52.220
and definitely on the promo, too,

00:24:52.300 --> 00:24:53.700
the computer gets in on a lot, too,

00:24:53.800 --> 00:24:56.040
but these are very high-scoring games

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:58.140
that are kind of, like, ridiculous, you know?

00:24:58.260 --> 00:24:59.120
But they're really long. And maybe we had...

00:24:59.120 --> 00:25:00.740
Sorry. Yeah, and they are...

00:25:00.740 --> 00:25:01.700
No, you're right, Sam.

00:25:01.820 --> 00:25:04.020
They are long in the sense of, like,

00:25:04.100 --> 00:25:06.820
if you're able to score that fast, right,

00:25:07.500 --> 00:25:09.720
then you kind of have to make the game shorter

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:12.220
so that the scores somewhat reflect that.

00:25:12.340 --> 00:25:14.660
Like, there's no way that in playtesting this,

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:15.880
they said, like,

00:25:15.880 --> 00:25:18.160
oh, final score was 13 to 9.

00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:19.540
Like, ship it.

00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:20.880
They definitely tried.

00:25:21.360 --> 00:25:23.780
Like, the clock does start at 20 minutes

00:25:23.780 --> 00:25:24.720
and counts down,

00:25:24.760 --> 00:25:27.680
but it's very accelerated,

00:25:28.340 --> 00:25:30.640
so they end up being what I imagine

00:25:30.640 --> 00:25:32.060
are five-minute periods.

00:25:33.160 --> 00:25:35.620
But I also don't think that at this point,

00:25:36.440 --> 00:25:39.300
they're not really going for simulation yet.

00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:45.060
Like, this isn't NHL 2K or NHL Live

00:25:45.060 --> 00:25:47.760
or NHL whatever.

00:25:48.740 --> 00:25:51.340
No, totally. And, you know, sorry,

00:25:51.420 --> 00:25:52.800
I'm jumping in on your thought here,

00:25:52.820 --> 00:25:54.300
but, like, I agree with that,

00:25:54.360 --> 00:25:56.400
like, that these games are supposed to be fun and arcadey,

00:25:56.400 --> 00:25:57.780
so I guess what I'm saying is,

00:25:57.800 --> 00:25:59.340
is just make them even shorter, though.

00:25:59.560 --> 00:26:02.020
Like, I'm not saying it should be hard to score goals.

00:26:02.260 --> 00:26:04.560
Like, that's the fun part of playing hockey, right,

00:26:04.600 --> 00:26:05.780
is putting the puck in the net.

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:09.740
So, like, if the games come out to 13 to 9,

00:26:09.860 --> 00:26:13.200
like I suggested, like, just make it so that the game lengths are shorter,

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:16.320
and, like, I'm sure that that's not as easy as, like,

00:26:16.600 --> 00:26:18.860
you know, flipping a switch.

00:26:19.120 --> 00:26:20.700
Yeah, yeah, yeah. Like, just shortening the games,

00:26:20.740 --> 00:26:22.520
I'm sure there would be some penalties from that, too.

00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:25.420
But if I had a comfortable lead,

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:27.260
like, when I played my first match,

00:26:27.360 --> 00:26:28.320
it was just, like, immediately.

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:30.320
I was winning, like, 9-0,

00:26:30.320 --> 00:26:32.900
and I was like, what's the point at this point

00:26:32.900 --> 00:26:34.140
of scoring any more goals?

00:26:34.140 --> 00:26:36.260
I might as well just run the timer out,

00:26:36.320 --> 00:26:37.800
and I was just passing around and whatever.

00:26:38.300 --> 00:26:39.620
And then I noticed, like, this is,

00:26:39.700 --> 00:26:41.280
but I'm saying, like, that takes a while.

00:26:41.520 --> 00:26:42.820
Yeah. Just passing the puck around

00:26:42.820 --> 00:26:44.840
and, like, avoiding the other players

00:26:44.840 --> 00:26:46.620
is, like, you realize,

00:26:46.840 --> 00:26:48.760
wow, this is a pretty lengthy clock

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:49.980
for the situation.

00:26:50.320 --> 00:26:53.020
Yes, it is not 20 full minutes of each period,

00:26:53.200 --> 00:26:54.840
but it's long for the situation

00:26:54.840 --> 00:26:55.720
of what you're playing.

00:26:55.760 --> 00:26:56.640
I totally agree.

00:26:56.820 --> 00:26:57.960
I mean, I wish that there was some option

00:26:57.960 --> 00:26:59.600
to, like, say, hey, let's just do it

00:26:59.600 --> 00:27:01.680
two-minute periods or something like that.

00:27:02.160 --> 00:28:04.120
No, I agree. No, I agree.

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:07.180
No, I agree.

00:28:07.180 --> 00:28:13.880
No, I agree.

00:28:13.880 --> 00:28:19.640
No, I agree.

00:28:24.060 --> 00:28:25.120
So we're all in agreement there.

00:28:25.320 --> 00:28:29.280
We need to talk about how the presentation of the games,

00:28:29.340 --> 00:28:31.600
that's always something that we're interested in as well.

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:34.680
I forget which game had, oh, it was soccer,

00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:37.860
had, like, a whole halftime show with cheerleaders

00:28:37.860 --> 00:28:39.320
and everything, and we really enjoyed that,

00:28:39.340 --> 00:28:40.920
and ever since then, we've been talking about

00:28:40.920 --> 00:28:42.380
the presentation of these games.

00:28:43.180 --> 00:28:44.720
Obviously, this game, you know,

00:28:44.720 --> 00:28:48.280
isn't going for the way the game is loaded.

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:49.580
It's not going for the way the game is loaded presents itself,

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:51.980
where it actually, like, tries to, like, fake out, like,

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:55.980
how the camera looks in an actual televised baseball game.

00:28:56.380 --> 00:29:00.960
But there is some nice flair here with the way that the players come out

00:29:00.960 --> 00:29:06.340
and with the half at the end of the periods where you go to the scoreboard.

00:29:06.780 --> 00:29:11.060
There's even a chance to play Gradius in this.

00:29:11.860 --> 00:29:13.560
That's something I came across.

00:29:13.900 --> 00:29:16.280
Like, it seems kind of random whether you get that

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:17.780
or you get, like, the commercial.

00:29:19.060 --> 00:29:21.060
But that was a surprise.

00:29:22.480 --> 00:29:27.160
But, no, I mean, obviously, you really can't top bases loaded right now yet.

00:29:28.160 --> 00:29:33.100
But I did appreciate that they showed the Jumbotron.

00:29:33.100 --> 00:29:35.400
You saw these goofy animations.

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:40.960
The celebrations for goals are a little bit more believable now

00:29:40.960 --> 00:29:44.600
than they were in ice hockey because, you know,

00:29:44.660 --> 00:29:46.420
they're all kind of gathering around.

00:29:46.420 --> 00:29:52.920
And they even have, like, what I imagine is the equivalent of the pregame warmup

00:29:52.920 --> 00:29:56.160
of just, like, you know, they're all just skating around their zone.

00:29:57.600 --> 00:29:59.340
And, hey, at least now there's, like,

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:02.300
we didn't actually mention that it's five on five now.

00:30:02.340 --> 00:30:04.600
So that's also a bit more realistic.

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:09.180
So, no, I did appreciate the presentation in this game.

00:30:09.260 --> 00:30:12.920
It's not the best in any sports game that we've played,

00:30:12.980 --> 00:30:14.480
but it's getting there.

00:30:15.500 --> 00:30:21.240
I got to go back to the fact that you can play another game in this game

00:30:21.240 --> 00:30:23.700
because I feel like that's just, like,

00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:26.480
that's, like, a level of, like, care about the audience.

00:30:26.640 --> 00:30:29.180
They're just like, oh, well, you know, let's give them a little break

00:30:29.180 --> 00:30:31.980
and actually put a whole other tiny little game.

00:30:31.980 --> 00:30:34.080
And, of course, it's a shmup, which nobody wants,

00:30:34.180 --> 00:30:37.880
but it is Gradius, so that's a plus.

00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:39.840
It's on our essential games list.

00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:45.260
Yeah, right, right. So, like, it was kind of cool that they didn't need to include that.

00:30:45.380 --> 00:30:48.640
It probably wouldn't have made much of a difference to them,

00:30:48.680 --> 00:30:50.380
but that's a nice little touch.

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:53.080
Yeah. It should have had, like, a, you know,

00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:55.120
buy it now at the end of that.

00:30:55.260 --> 00:30:57.600
You know, like, it went so close to being a commercial

00:30:57.600 --> 00:30:59.280
that they might as well have just said, like,

00:30:59.340 --> 00:31:01.240
available at a store near you.

00:31:01.240 --> 00:31:05.140
Yeah. Because they also flash up, like, ads for Jackal.

00:31:05.380 --> 00:31:07.360
And Contra. And Contra, yeah.

00:31:07.540 --> 00:31:09.100
So, like, why not, you know?

00:31:09.320 --> 00:31:10.780
I think they should have just been all these.

00:31:10.880 --> 00:31:13.320
But obviously those are all Konami games, so it makes sense.

00:31:13.420 --> 00:31:15.380
It should link you to the Nintendo store.

00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:17.360
You should be able to go right there, click on it,

00:31:17.540 --> 00:31:19.040
go right to the Nintendo store on your NES,

00:31:19.200 --> 00:31:21.680
and then you get the Wii Shop channel.

00:31:22.020 --> 00:31:23.200
Yeah, the Wii eShop.

00:31:24.220 --> 00:31:25.680
Everyone loves shopping on it every Wednesday.

00:31:25.780 --> 00:31:34.140
Because it's Wednesday. And then, Sean, you know, the tying system here,

00:31:34.160 --> 00:31:38.780
I'm a little confused because this seems kind of similar

00:31:38.780 --> 00:31:41.520
to how we handle ties in hockey now,

00:31:41.520 --> 00:31:44.800
but I don't think this was the case in, like, 1988, right?

00:31:44.940 --> 00:31:46.300
Did they do shootouts?

00:31:46.880 --> 00:31:53.800
So, I don't know the exact context of where this was used,

00:31:53.800 --> 00:31:57.360
but in some level of high-level hockey,

00:31:57.520 --> 00:31:59.860
it may have even been international hockey,

00:32:00.440 --> 00:32:02.620
they didn't just go to overtime.

00:32:02.780 --> 00:32:05.360
I think there were situations where it went to shootouts

00:32:05.360 --> 00:32:12.740
before the NHL decided to do that in, like, I think 2006, 2007.

00:32:14.500 --> 00:32:16.740
But, no, this has precedent.

00:32:17.020 --> 00:32:20.900
Like, there was some level of hockey that did go to shootout after a tie.

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:23.680
It is weird that there's no overtime beforehand,

00:32:24.640 --> 00:32:27.640
but, no, it's not completely goofball.

00:32:28.040 --> 00:32:31.440
Yeah, and I like that it actually forces you to decide a win or two.

00:32:31.600 --> 00:32:34.680
It's not just, like, after the five shots.

00:32:35.100 --> 00:32:37.040
Yeah, like, oh, tie it after that.

00:32:37.160 --> 00:32:40.960
Like, whatever. It's like, no, somebody must win, and we will be here all night.

00:32:41.260 --> 00:32:46.940
Yeah. So, we have Double Dribble, Track and Field, now Blades of Steel.

00:32:47.180 --> 00:32:50.620
These are the Konami sports games that we've played so far.

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:52.960
How do we feel about Konami?

00:32:53.260 --> 00:32:54.460
I mean, we love that developer,

00:32:54.640 --> 00:32:56.420
but how do we feel about their sports games output?

00:32:56.800 --> 00:32:58.540
If I remember correctly, I think we all...

00:32:58.540 --> 00:32:59.800
We loved that developer.

00:33:00.440 --> 00:33:03.780
Yeah. Go ahead. I think we all were pretty high on Double Dribble, right?

00:33:04.420 --> 00:33:05.560
Yeah, I like Double Dribble a lot.

00:33:05.560 --> 00:33:07.480
I'm high on all three of these games.

00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:14.000
No, I do think that these are very competent sports titles for the time.

00:33:14.220 --> 00:33:16.900
I think that Blades of Steel, I'm high on that, too.

00:33:17.500 --> 00:33:21.720
But comparing it to ice hockey,

00:33:21.840 --> 00:33:23.620
where it has some things that it does better,

00:33:23.720 --> 00:33:25.100
and it is nice that it's five on five,

00:33:25.180 --> 00:33:28.740
it does a few things that it does better. I think, like, now looking at them both so close together,

00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:30.960
I think I'd probably go ice hockey still.

00:33:31.380 --> 00:33:34.940
I still think that's maybe my favorite ice hockey game on the NES so far,

00:33:34.940 --> 00:33:36.700
just because there's a little more of choice here.

00:33:36.960 --> 00:33:38.000
Chill, Joe. Chill.

00:33:38.220 --> 00:33:39.860
Okay? I know that wasn't the question.

00:33:40.540 --> 00:33:42.360
Yeah, yeah. We're just talking about the Konami sports games.

00:33:42.480 --> 00:33:43.680
We're not in the EGL yet.

00:33:43.760 --> 00:33:44.700
Right. But I'm just...

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:46.400
That's why this...

00:33:46.780 --> 00:33:48.240
As far as the Konami sports games,

00:33:48.580 --> 00:33:50.700
I go Double Dribble the highest

00:33:51.140 --> 00:33:52.720
because now when I'm playing Blades of Steel,

00:33:52.840 --> 00:33:54.400
I'm thinking, well, there's another hockey game out

00:33:54.400 --> 00:33:56.120
that it's not made by Konami that I can do.

00:33:56.260 --> 00:33:57.640
The question, of course, is, though,

00:33:57.780 --> 00:34:00.460
is which sports game should Konami make next?

00:34:00.620 --> 00:34:04.340
Like, would you want them to refine Double Dribble

00:34:04.340 --> 00:34:06.620
or Blades of Steel and try to make another one of those games?

00:34:06.700 --> 00:34:08.740
Or would you say, like, no, those games are good enough.

00:34:08.840 --> 00:34:09.840
Like, you did what you could.

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:11.500
Now I want to see baseball.

00:34:11.640 --> 00:34:12.640
Now I want to see soccer.

00:34:12.820 --> 00:34:14.260
Like, just keep doing every sport.

00:34:14.440 --> 00:34:17.180
I mean, their track record up to this point,

00:34:17.340 --> 00:34:20.520
and, I mean, I'm kind of in the same situation

00:34:20.520 --> 00:34:22.000
as somebody in 88 would be

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:24.820
because I don't know any more Konami sports games.

00:34:26.500 --> 00:34:28.680
I'd like to see them take a crack at football.

00:34:29.120 --> 00:34:31.700
I'd like to see them take a crack at soccer.

00:34:32.060 --> 00:34:34.980
But I also would like to see what they can come up with

00:34:34.980 --> 00:34:36.900
with the sports that they've already touched upon

00:34:36.900 --> 00:34:40.940
because they have proven that they're at least making fun games.

00:34:41.080 --> 00:34:45.380
They may not be the most to the letter of the rule book,

00:34:46.980 --> 00:34:48.520
but they are fun.

00:34:48.840 --> 00:34:51.960
I'd like to see them do some kind of downhill skiing,

00:34:52.240 --> 00:34:53.760
snowboarding kind of game.

00:34:53.760 --> 00:34:55.060
I think they would do that well.

00:34:55.600 --> 00:34:56.880
Their take on slalom.

00:34:56.880 --> 00:34:58.960
Yeah, their take on slalom, I bet, would be really good.

00:34:59.140 --> 00:35:00.660
Probably be essential. Probably.

00:35:01.460 --> 00:35:07.280
Yeah. The next Konami sports game is Skate or Die.

00:35:07.760 --> 00:35:11.640
So it may be controversial that I'm listing

00:35:11.640 --> 00:35:15.240
an extreme sport as a sports game.

00:35:15.440 --> 00:35:16.600
Sports in the word, though.

00:35:16.600 --> 00:35:18.000
You're good. Yeah, sports in the word.

00:35:18.280 --> 00:35:22.200
I always thought that skateboarding was always just a real sport, too.

00:35:22.200 --> 00:35:24.940
And maybe it's because I grew up around Tony Hawk's pro skater time

00:35:24.940 --> 00:35:26.080
where it just became normalized.

00:35:26.380 --> 00:35:31.900
But, like, anything that involves physically scoring points with your body

00:35:31.900 --> 00:35:33.860
to me sounds like a sport.

00:35:34.180 --> 00:35:35.380
It does. Yeah.

00:35:35.380 --> 00:35:38.720
So if we have nothing else to talk about Blades of Steel,

00:35:39.120 --> 00:35:43.100
I think it's time that we, you know, we make a decision on Blades of Steel.

00:35:43.460 --> 00:35:44.420
Sean, is that okay with you?

00:35:45.080 --> 00:35:47.320
Uh, I can, I can abide.

00:35:47.700 --> 00:35:48.960
Okay. Sean can abide.

00:35:49.180 --> 00:35:55.020
So you, the listener, will abide us in the Essential Games list.

00:35:57.800 --> 00:35:58.780
Sam? Hey.

00:35:59.020 --> 00:36:02.260
I don't know what I'm going to say next.

00:36:02.280 --> 00:36:04.020
And that makes me very dangerous.

00:36:04.280 --> 00:36:06.760
So I need you to vote first.

00:36:06.900 --> 00:36:11.780
Okay. Uh, this is, this is a fun sports game.

00:36:12.940 --> 00:36:14.420
I enjoyed it.

00:36:14.580 --> 00:36:20.260
I think that it's, for what it is, it has a goal and it accomplishes it very well.

00:36:20.260 --> 00:36:25.980
And I feel like, especially if I had a friend to play it with, I think I'd have a really good time.

00:36:26.140 --> 00:36:27.700
But, I don't know.

00:36:27.840 --> 00:36:31.740
I feel like if you're going to play a sports game these days, it's not this one.

00:36:31.980 --> 00:36:34.240
This ain't it. So, not Essential.

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:37.700
Still a good, solid game, but not Essential.

00:36:37.960 --> 00:36:38.900
Well said.

00:36:39.600 --> 00:36:45.280
Joe? Well, since, I mean, you pulled us aside before the show and mentioned that Konami didn't pay us to make this Essential.

00:36:45.480 --> 00:36:47.040
So I'm going to say no, not Essential.

00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:52.540
No, but in all seriousness, I'll say that I think that it's similar.

00:36:52.720 --> 00:36:56.660
I mean, it's a perfectly fine hockey game.

00:36:57.360 --> 00:37:01.860
But there's just, I hate to fall into that same thing we say about all the sports games.

00:37:01.980 --> 00:37:06.040
But there's just too many other versions of hockey you can play that are better.

00:37:06.220 --> 00:37:08.160
Even one that I think maybe we already played.

00:37:08.420 --> 00:37:10.380
So I'm going to have to say not Essential.

00:37:10.660 --> 00:37:12.580
All right. And I will go next.

00:37:12.760 --> 00:37:21.120
Sorry, Sean. And I will just say that something Sam said during his Essential games list where he like, you know, he had fun.

00:37:21.500 --> 00:37:23.400
It does what it sets out to do.

00:37:23.720 --> 00:37:25.420
I agree. 100%.

00:37:25.420 --> 00:37:27.520
I could see myself playing this game again.

00:37:27.860 --> 00:37:30.060
That doesn't mean it's on the Essential games list.

00:37:30.060 --> 00:37:37.800
And I don't know what it will take for one of these traditional sports games.

00:37:37.900 --> 00:37:42.280
Like, say, a hockey, a basketball, a football, a baseball game.

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:45.040
I don't know what it will take to get one of them on the Essential games list.

00:37:45.440 --> 00:37:47.960
Because this one was a lot of fun.

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:49.600
And yet it's still not there.

00:37:49.600 --> 00:37:52.040
And I don't know why that is.

00:37:52.160 --> 00:38:11.640
But I think that's because gamers, right, don't need the recommendation from us on whether they should play these kinds of games when the possibility of playing hockey has been simulated across many different systems and many different years in many different ways.

00:38:12.140 --> 00:38:17.820
I'm not sure that I would put my foot down and say, if you've got to play one hockey game, then I know which one it is.

00:38:17.980 --> 00:38:20.140
I mean, some people would tell you NHL 94.

00:38:20.460 --> 00:38:22.500
But I might say Wayne Gretzky's 3D hockey.

00:38:22.800 --> 00:38:23.720
And somebody might play it.

00:38:23.720 --> 00:38:26.760
No, it's actually just the latest NHL 2K blah blah.

00:38:27.060 --> 00:38:31.680
You know? Like, it's always everybody's going to have a different opinion on these kinds of sports games.

00:38:31.720 --> 00:38:36.620
And there's just so many of them that I think the breadth of possibilities is too large.

00:38:36.620 --> 00:38:40.820
So that's why I keep having this internal issue about what to do with these sports games.

00:38:40.980 --> 00:38:42.300
They're fun, and I'm enjoying them.

00:38:42.420 --> 00:38:46.280
I just don't quite get to that upper echelon of video games.

00:38:46.560 --> 00:38:47.860
Sean? Well, yeah.

00:38:48.100 --> 00:38:50.600
I mean, I agree with that sentiment.

00:38:51.880 --> 00:38:53.900
I'm not putting this on the Essential Games list.

00:38:54.000 --> 00:38:54.880
I'll get that out of the way.

00:38:54.880 --> 00:39:09.480
But I, as a sports game, I guess, fetishist, will just bring up that I kind of have my own little essential sports game list in the NES era.

00:39:09.880 --> 00:39:12.340
And I'm actually going to put this at the top of that.

00:39:12.800 --> 00:39:19.000
Like, this, if so, I mean, it's definitely not the most perfectly executed sports game.

00:39:19.000 --> 00:39:25.800
I'm not saying that this is better than any of them in that right, though it is better than most of them.

00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:42.620
But just by the implication of what they've done to the rules with the fights and how they enforce that and, like, the little changes in their presentation and just the fact that I am, by and large, a hockey fan.

00:39:42.620 --> 00:39:45.700
And I do really enjoy this game.

00:39:45.880 --> 00:39:58.720
And, again, if you just, and I say this after every one of them, if you have any curiosity as to how sports games were sort of interpreted in this period, play this game.

00:39:58.880 --> 00:40:03.260
It's fun and it's weird and it's mostly because it's weird.

00:40:03.480 --> 00:40:04.860
But, yeah, play this game.

00:40:05.280 --> 00:40:06.920
Not essential to play it.

00:40:07.160 --> 00:40:12.480
Sean, you'll have to sometime soon publish your list of these sports games.

00:40:12.620 --> 00:40:15.860
Maybe I will. I would like to see someone on the Internet.

00:40:16.020 --> 00:40:17.460
It doesn't have to be you, Sean, but why not?

00:40:17.920 --> 00:40:21.260
Just somebody rank all the hockey games that ever came out.

00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:25.480
Oh, man, that was a lot that I haven't played, especially Wayne Gretzky's 3D Hockey.

00:40:26.140 --> 00:40:27.960
You've never played Wayne Gretzky's 3D Hockey?

00:40:28.100 --> 00:40:29.640
I have not. Wow.

00:40:29.900 --> 00:40:31.760
Robin Hood and his merry men are at it again.

00:40:33.040 --> 00:40:35.020
Anyway, just something.

00:40:35.400 --> 00:40:39.880
You remember how we were saying about, like, well, you know, the pass thing?

00:40:40.120 --> 00:40:41.860
Shoot the pass or whatever, get the pass.

00:40:41.860 --> 00:40:42.720
Yes, but it's a pass.

00:40:43.020 --> 00:40:45.960
Okay. That's what they say over and over again.

00:40:46.020 --> 00:40:49.640
That's what the announcer says over and over again in Wayne Gretzky's 3D Hockey.

00:40:49.820 --> 00:40:52.240
He just keeps saying, Robin Hood and his merry men are at it again.

00:40:52.380 --> 00:40:54.760
And it's like, what does that mean to anybody?

00:40:54.760 --> 00:41:04.080
My favorite one of those is, I think it was NFL 2K5 or before that.

00:41:04.400 --> 00:41:08.580
But it was said after, like, a quarterback was sacked.

00:41:08.980 --> 00:41:17.220
And announcer A would say to announcer B, he'd say, man, he was groceries right there.

00:41:17.660 --> 00:41:19.420
Get it? Sacked.

00:41:20.200 --> 00:41:21.840
And it was my favorite thing.

00:41:22.040 --> 00:41:24.460
And they said it five times a game.

00:41:24.720 --> 00:41:26.180
It was, okay. Anyway, I'm sorry.

00:41:26.580 --> 00:41:31.780
Sean, NFL 2K5, that's the one where you can, like, have your own crib, right?

00:41:31.780 --> 00:41:33.080
That's the crib game, yeah.

00:41:33.440 --> 00:41:34.480
I love that game.

00:41:34.500 --> 00:41:36.060
It's a great game. Yeah.

00:41:36.640 --> 00:41:38.440
MVP Baseball 2005 also.

00:41:38.440 --> 00:41:44.900
Those are two great sports games that I would consider over modern games.

00:41:45.140 --> 00:41:45.940
Even the show?

00:41:46.960 --> 00:41:48.040
No, no, no, no.

00:41:48.140 --> 00:41:53.900
The show is great. But in the show, I don't know the most recent version of the show.

00:41:54.020 --> 00:41:59.480
But in MVP Baseball, you could actually decide what day was bobblehead games at the stadium.

00:42:01.000 --> 00:42:03.660
You can do that in the most recent NHL games.

00:42:03.840 --> 00:42:05.800
But I get that, yeah.

00:42:06.320 --> 00:42:07.600
You know, like, that was just cool.

00:42:07.600 --> 00:42:10.440
And I was like, and I'm going to charge $20 for those bobbles.

00:42:10.820 --> 00:42:12.180
That's perfect. I love it.

00:42:12.180 --> 00:42:14.780
I'd throw in the Backyard Sports series also.

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:17.580
That's sacrilege. They had a hockey game.

00:42:17.700 --> 00:42:19.380
It was good. No, I'm kidding.

00:42:19.420 --> 00:42:20.700
That's fun. I like those games.

00:42:21.400 --> 00:42:23.600
Was, like, was Sidney Crosby in that?

00:42:23.700 --> 00:42:24.560
Like, who would have been the star?

00:42:24.680 --> 00:42:27.360
Sidney Crosby. No, what was the kid that was good at everything?

00:42:27.500 --> 00:42:29.220
Isn't there, like, one kid who had, like, highest stats?

00:42:29.760 --> 00:42:31.220
Yes, that's right. Pablo Sanchez.

00:42:33.180 --> 00:42:34.400
Well, I'll tell you what.

00:42:34.400 --> 00:42:40.480
Pablo Sanchez was not needed in Blades of Steel because you can just easily score on your opponent.

00:42:41.160 --> 00:42:42.320
Everybody's Pablo Sanchez.

00:42:42.460 --> 00:42:43.840
Yeah, you needed him on the other team.

00:42:44.240 --> 00:42:45.040
Yeah. All right.

00:42:45.180 --> 00:42:50.080
Well, next week, it's not quite a sports game, but involves driving a car.

00:42:50.200 --> 00:42:53.700
And I guess you could make the argument that I'm about to introduce, like, a NASCAR game.

